<Layout
  title="Which Home Improvements Are Worth Making Before You Sell?"
  description="Not every renovation raises the selling price. Which improvements help a Temecula or Southern California home show well, and which ones are not worth the cost."
>
  <Navbar />
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 sm:px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
    <p class="text-[11px] tracking-[0.22em] uppercase text-[var(--sage)] font-medium">October 30, 2026 · Sellers</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl sm:text-5xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] mt-3 leading-tight">Which Home Improvements Are Worth Making Before You Sell? A Seller’s Guide to Spending Wisely</h1>
    <article class="mt-8 space-y-4 text-[#2B322C] leading-relaxed text-base">
      <p>When preparing to sell a home, one of the most common questions homeowners face is how much money they should invest in improvements before putting the property on the market.</p>
      <p>Should you remodel the kitchen? Replace the flooring? Update the bathrooms? Repaint the entire interior? Or simply clean, declutter, and let the next owner make their own changes?</p>
      <p>The answer depends on several factors, including the home’s current condition, its location, competing properties, buyer expectations, and the amount of time and money available for preparation.</p>
      <p>While certain improvements can make a property more appealing, not every renovation translates into a higher selling price. In some cases, an expensive upgrade may offer little additional benefit, particularly when buyers have different preferences or the surrounding market does not support the investment.</p>
      <p>For homeowners in Temecula and throughout Southern California, the smartest approach is to evaluate each potential improvement based on its cost, visibility, practicality, and likely appeal to prospective buyers.</p>
      <p>The objective is simple: make thoughtful improvements that help your home show well without spending unnecessarily.</p>
      <ArticleContact line="Before you spend on improvements, contact me." />

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">1. Start With the Most Important Question: What Will Buyers Notice?</h2>
      <p>Before deciding which projects to undertake, consider how prospective buyers will experience your home.</p>
      <p>Most buyers begin forming an impression almost immediately. They notice the exterior, the condition of the entrance, the overall cleanliness, the smell of the home, and whether the interior feels bright and inviting.</p>
      <p>As they walk through the property, they begin evaluating the condition of the kitchen, bathrooms, bedrooms, flooring, storage, and major systems.</p>
      <p>A home does not need to be newly renovated to attract interest. However, visible neglect can cause buyers to question how well the property has been maintained.</p>
      <p>Walk through your home as though you were seeing it for the first time.</p>
      <p>Ask yourself:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Does the exterior look welcoming and well maintained?</li>
        <li>Are the walls, floors, and surfaces clean?</li>
        <li>Do the kitchen and bathrooms appear cared for?</li>
        <li>Are there obvious repairs that have been postponed?</li>
        <li>Does each room feel spacious and functional?</li>
        <li>Is there sufficient lighting?</li>
        <li>Are there unpleasant odors or distracting personal belongings?</li>
        <li>Does the property feel move-in ready, even if some finishes are dated?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This exercise can help identify improvements that deserve attention before considering larger projects.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">2. Fresh Paint: One of the Most Practical Improvements</h2>
      <p>A fresh coat of paint can make a home feel cleaner, brighter, and better maintained.</p>
      <p>It is often one of the more practical cosmetic improvements because it can address visible wear without requiring a major renovation.</p>
      <p>Consider repainting areas with:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Scuffed or marked walls.</li>
        <li>Faded or discolored paint.</li>
        <li>Noticeable patchwork.</li>
        <li>Bold colors that may distract prospective buyers.</li>
        <li>Damaged trim or baseboards.</li>
        <li>Peeling or deteriorating finishes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When selecting colors, consider soft whites, warm neutrals, and understated tones that complement the home’s architecture and existing finishes.</p>
      <p>The objective is to create a cohesive appearance that allows buyers to focus on the property rather than the seller’s personal decorating preferences.</p>
      <p>However, repainting every room is not always necessary. If the existing paint is in good condition and the colors are broadly appealing, a thorough cleaning and a few touch-ups may be sufficient.</p>
      <p>The bottom line: Fresh paint is often worth considering when visible wear makes the home look older or less maintained than it actually is.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">3. Kitchen Updates: Refresh Rather Than Automatically Remodel</h2>
      <p>The kitchen is one of the most closely evaluated areas of a home.</p>
      <p>Buyers often pay attention to cabinet condition, countertop materials, appliances, storage, lighting, and the overall layout. A dated kitchen can make an otherwise attractive property feel older, but that does not necessarily mean a complete remodel is justified.</p>
      <p>Before committing to a major renovation, consider smaller improvements.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Cabinet Refreshing</h3>
      <p>If the existing cabinets are structurally sound, replacing them may be unnecessary.</p>
      <p>Depending on their condition and style, options may include:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Professional cabinet painting or refinishing.</li>
        <li>Replacing outdated handles and pulls.</li>
        <li>Adjusting loose hinges.</li>
        <li>Repairing damaged cabinet doors.</li>
        <li>Improving interior organization.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These changes can help give the kitchen a more coordinated appearance without the expense of replacing the entire cabinetry system.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Countertops and Backsplashes</h3>
      <p>Damaged or heavily dated countertops may affect the kitchen’s overall presentation.</p>
      <p>If replacement is being considered, select materials and colors that complement the rest of the kitchen rather than choosing something highly personalized.</p>
      <p>A backsplash that is damaged or visibly outdated may also warrant attention, particularly when it creates an inconsistent appearance.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Appliances</h3>
      <p>If appliances are clean, functional, and reasonably consistent, replacing them simply for the sake of listing the property may not be necessary.</p>
      <p>However, visibly damaged, unreliable, or severely mismatched appliances may detract from the presentation.</p>
      <p>Buyers should receive accurate information about the condition of appliances included in the sale.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Kitchen Lighting</h3>
      <p>Lighting can make a meaningful difference in how a kitchen feels.</p>
      <p>Consider replacing dated fixtures, ensuring all bulbs work, and improving illumination in darker areas.</p>
      <p>Under-cabinet lighting may also be worth considering when it complements the existing design.</p>
      <p>The bottom line: A kitchen refresh can be worthwhile when it addresses visible wear or creates a more cohesive appearance. A complete remodel should be evaluated carefully against its cost and the expectations of competing properties.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">4. Bathroom Improvements That Make a Difference</h2>
      <p>Bathrooms are another area where buyers tend to notice condition and cleanliness.</p>
      <p>A bathroom does not necessarily need expensive new tile or fixtures to make a positive impression.</p>
      <p>Start with the basics:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Deep-clean tile and grout.</li>
        <li>Remove hard-water deposits where appropriate.</li>
        <li>Repair dripping faucets.</li>
        <li>Replace damaged caulking.</li>
        <li>Address visible plumbing leaks.</li>
        <li>Clean or replace worn shower hardware.</li>
        <li>Ensure exhaust fans function properly.</li>
        <li>Replace outdated or damaged light fixtures.</li>
        <li>Organize vanity storage.</li>
        <li>Repair or replace damaged mirrors.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If the vanity is in good condition, replacing its hardware or updating the faucet may provide a refreshed appearance.</p>
      <p>For bathrooms with visibly damaged flooring, deteriorating surfaces, or evidence of water intrusion, professional evaluation may be appropriate before listing.</p>
      <p>Avoid covering up moisture problems with cosmetic materials. Any known material condition should be handled and disclosed as required.</p>
      <p>The bottom line: A clean, functional, well-maintained bathroom can be more valuable to the overall presentation than an expensive cosmetic renovation.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">5. Flooring: Repair, Refresh, or Replace?</h2>
      <p>Flooring covers a substantial amount of visual space, so its condition can influence how buyers perceive the entire property.</p>
      <p>Worn carpets, damaged flooring, loose tiles, or inconsistent finishes may distract from otherwise attractive rooms.</p>
      <p>Before replacing flooring throughout the home, evaluate what is actually needed.</p>
      <p>Possible solutions include:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Professional carpet cleaning.</li>
        <li>Repairing damaged sections where practical.</li>
        <li>Refinishing hardwood floors.</li>
        <li>Replacing heavily stained or worn carpeting.</li>
        <li>Repairing loose or damaged tiles.</li>
        <li>Addressing damaged transitions between flooring materials.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If replacement is necessary, consider how the new material will work with the home’s existing finishes and architectural style.</p>
      <p>Neutral, broadly appealing flooring can help create visual continuity, particularly when several rooms connect. However, replacing perfectly serviceable flooring simply because it is not the seller’s preferred style may be an unnecessary expense.</p>
      <p>The bottom line: Address flooring that looks damaged, dirty, or poorly maintained. Replace it when cleaning or repair is unlikely to achieve an acceptable presentation.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">6. Curb Appeal: An Investment Buyers Notice Immediately</h2>
      <p>The exterior of a property creates the first impression, both when buyers arrive and when they view the listing online.</p>
      <p>A well-maintained exterior can make a home feel inviting before a prospective buyer even steps inside.</p>
      <p>Fortunately, many curb appeal improvements are relatively straightforward.</p>
      <p>Consider:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Trimming shrubs and trees.</li>
        <li>Removing weeds and dead vegetation.</li>
        <li>Adding fresh mulch where appropriate.</li>
        <li>Cleaning walkways and driveways.</li>
        <li>Washing the front door and exterior surfaces.</li>
        <li>Cleaning or replacing worn exterior light fixtures.</li>
        <li>Refreshing the front entrance.</li>
        <li>Replacing a damaged or outdated doormat.</li>
        <li>Adding a few attractive planters.</li>
        <li>Ensuring the house numbers are visible and presentable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In Temecula, landscaping deserves particular attention.</p>
      <p>Summer heat and water requirements make it important to maintain irrigation systems, remove dead vegetation, and select appropriate plants.</p>
      <p>A thoughtfully maintained landscape can be attractive without requiring extensive water use.</p>
      <p>If the home has a covered entry, attractive landscaping, or a particularly inviting front porch, make sure those features are showcased.</p>
      <p>The bottom line: Curb appeal is often a sensible place to invest because it influences the buyer’s first impression and improves the property’s overall presentation.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">7. Landscaping and Backyard Improvements</h2>
      <p>For many Southern California buyers, the backyard is an important part of the home’s appeal.</p>
      <p>Outdoor living spaces can provide opportunities for entertaining, relaxing, gardening, and enjoying the climate.</p>
      <p>However, a backyard does not need to resemble an elaborate resort to be attractive.</p>
      <p>Focus on making the space feel clean, usable, and well maintained.</p>
      <p>Potential improvements include:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Cleaning patios and outdoor surfaces.</li>
        <li>Trimming overgrown plants.</li>
        <li>Removing dead or unhealthy vegetation.</li>
        <li>Repairing damaged irrigation components.</li>
        <li>Adding shade where practical.</li>
        <li>Organizing outdoor furniture.</li>
        <li>Cleaning pool decks and visible pool equipment.</li>
        <li>Refreshing existing planting beds.</li>
        <li>Improving outdoor lighting.</li>
        <li>Removing unnecessary storage items.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If the property has a swimming pool, make sure it is properly maintained and that all required safety features remain in place.</p>
      <p>Avoid installing expensive outdoor features simply because they might appeal to a particular buyer. The return on such projects can be difficult to predict.</p>
      <p>A neat, inviting outdoor environment is often more important than an extensive collection of elaborate improvements.</p>
      <p>The bottom line: Make the backyard look like an enjoyable extension of the home, while avoiding expensive additions that may not be recovered in the sale.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">8. Lighting and Small Design Details</h2>
      <p>Lighting can dramatically influence how a property feels.</p>
      <p>Dark rooms may appear smaller or less inviting, while good lighting can highlight architectural details and create a more comfortable atmosphere.</p>
      <p>Before considering major changes, take a look at the existing fixtures and natural light.</p>
      <p>Useful improvements may include:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Replacing broken or dated light fixtures.</li>
        <li>Ensuring bulbs are working throughout the home.</li>
        <li>Cleaning windows and accessible window coverings.</li>
        <li>Using consistent light color temperatures.</li>
        <li>Removing heavy window coverings where appropriate.</li>
        <li>Adding lighting to darker areas.</li>
        <li>Replacing damaged switch plates and outlet covers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Small details matter, too.</p>
      <p>Loose cabinet handles, damaged door hardware, scratched trim, and visibly worn fixtures can collectively make a home feel neglected.</p>
      <p>Addressing these relatively minor issues can help create a more polished presentation. The bottom line: Good lighting and attention to detail can improve the overall impression without requiring substantial renovation expenses.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">9. Energy Efficiency and Major Home Systems</h2>
      <p>Buyers are often interested in the condition and operating costs of major home systems.</p>
      <p>In Temecula, where summer temperatures can become quite high, a properly functioning air-conditioning system is particularly important.</p>
      <p>Before listing, consider having major systems evaluated or serviced as appropriate.</p>
      <p>These may include:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Heating and air conditioning.</li>
        <li>Water heaters.</li>
        <li>Plumbing systems.</li>
        <li>Electrical systems.</li>
        <li>Roofing.</li>
        <li>Windows and exterior doors.</li>
        <li>Solar equipment, if applicable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Routine servicing and available maintenance records can help demonstrate that systems have received attention.</p>
      <p>If a major system is nearing the end of its useful life, discuss the situation with your real estate professional before deciding whether replacement is appropriate.</p>
      <p>Energy-efficient improvements may be attractive to buyers, but installing expensive equipment solely to increase a home’s selling price does not guarantee a corresponding return.</p>
      <p>Solar systems deserve particular attention because ownership, financing, leases, and transfer requirements can vary. Sellers should organize the applicable agreements and documentation before listing.</p>
      <p>The bottom line: Prioritize proper operation, safety, and maintenance. Evaluate major replacements individually rather than assuming every older system needs to be upgraded before selling.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">10. Should You Remodel Before Selling?</h2>
      <p>This is one of the most important financial decisions a homeowner can make.</p>
      <p>A complete kitchen renovation, bathroom remodel, new flooring throughout the home, or extensive exterior renovation can involve substantial expense.</p>
      <p>Although improvements may increase buyer appeal, the amount spent does not necessarily translate into an equivalent increase in the eventual selling price.</p>
      <p>Consider a simple example.</p>
      <p>A homeowner spends $35,000 remodeling a kitchen. The result is beautiful, but comparable homes in the neighborhood have similar selling prices whether their kitchens are updated or not.</p>
      <p>The homeowner may have improved the property’s appearance without recovering the full renovation expense.</p>
      <p>In another situation, a dated kitchen may be significantly less appealing than competing properties, and a carefully planned refresh may help the home compete more effectively.</p>
      <p>The difference lies in understanding the market and the property’s position within it.</p>
      <p>Before undertaking a major project, consider:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>What condition are competing homes in?</li>
        <li>Are buyers in this price range expecting updated finishes?</li>
        <li>Will the improvement address a genuine weakness?</li>
        <li>How much will the project cost?</li>
        <li>How long will the work take?</li>
        <li>Is there sufficient time before the planned listing date?</li>
        <li>Is there a less expensive way to accomplish a similar result?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A renovation should have a clear purpose rather than being undertaken simply because the homeowner believes every feature must be modernized before selling.</p>
      <ArticleContact line="Not sure which project is worth it? Contact me." />

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">11. Improvements That May Not Be Worth the Expense</h2>
      <p>Not every home improvement is a good investment immediately before a sale.</p>
      <p>Some projects may be attractive to the current owner but offer little additional appeal to prospective buyers.</p>
      <p>Examples include:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Highly personalized renovations:</strong> Unusual colors, elaborate decorative features, or specialized design choices may appeal to some buyers but not others.</li>
        <li><strong>Luxury upgrades in a market that does not support them:</strong> An expensive renovation may be difficult to justify if similar homes in the neighborhood do not command a corresponding premium.</li>
        <li><strong>Replacing functional materials unnecessarily:</strong> Perfectly serviceable countertops, flooring, or fixtures do not always need to be replaced.</li>
        <li><strong>Elaborate landscaping:</strong> Extensive gardens, expensive plantings, and complex outdoor installations may introduce additional maintenance rather than increasing broad buyer appeal.</li>
        <li><strong>Adding rooms without evaluating market expectations:</strong> Major additions can involve permitting, construction expenses, and lengthy timelines. Their value should be assessed before proceeding.</li>
        <li><strong>Installing a pool solely to sell the property:</strong> Pools can be attractive to some buyers but are not universally desirable. Installation costs and ongoing maintenance should be carefully evaluated.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The most effective preparation strategy is usually selective rather than excessive.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">12. Don’t Overlook Repairs and Property Maintenance</h2>
      <p>Cosmetic improvements can make a home look attractive, but they should not take priority over significant maintenance concerns.</p>
      <p>Buyers may arrange inspections and ask questions about the condition of the property.</p>
      <p>Issues involving roofing, plumbing, electrical systems, water intrusion, drainage, structural components, and other major systems should be taken seriously.</p>
      <p>If you know of a problem, do not simply cover it with paint, flooring, or decorative materials.</p>
      <p>Seek appropriate professional evaluation, determine what corrective work may be necessary, and understand your disclosure obligations.</p>
      <p>Addressing important maintenance concerns before listing can help reduce surprises during the transaction.</p>
      <p>It may also be useful to gather available invoices, permits, warranties, and maintenance records for completed work.</p>
      <p>Transparency and proper documentation are important parts of preparing a property for sale.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">13. How to Decide Where to Spend Your Money</h2>
      <p>One of the most effective ways to control preparation expenses is to create a prioritized improvement plan.</p>
      <p>Rather than approaching every possible project as equally important, divide the work into categories.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Priority One: Essential Repairs</h3>
      <p>These include issues involving safety, function, active leaks, significant damage, and other important maintenance concerns.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Priority Two: Presentation Improvements</h3>
      <p>These include cleaning, decluttering, painting where needed, landscaping, lighting, and correcting visible cosmetic problems.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Priority Three: Selective Upgrades</h3>
      <p>These may include cabinet refinishing, fixture replacement, flooring updates, or other improvements that could make the property more competitive.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl sm:text-2xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-4">Priority Four: Optional Renovations</h3>
      <p>These are larger projects that should only be considered when there is a compelling reason supported by the property’s condition and local market circumstances.</p>
      <p>This approach allows homeowners to make decisions in a logical order while avoiding unnecessary spending.</p>
      <p>It also makes it easier to establish a realistic preparation budget.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">14. The Importance of a Local Real Estate Market Evaluation</h2>
      <p>A home’s location, condition, and competition within the local market should influence preparation decisions.</p>
      <p>For example, a property in an established Temecula neighborhood with mostly original finishes may need a different preparation strategy from a home in a newer community where competing properties have more contemporary interiors.</p>
      <p>Similarly, a home with a desirable view, attractive outdoor living area, or extensive landscaping may benefit more from highlighting those features than from undertaking an expensive interior renovation.</p>
      <p>A local real estate professional can help evaluate:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
        <li>Recent comparable sales.</li>
        <li>Current competing listings.</li>
        <li>Buyer expectations in the property’s price range.</li>
        <li>The condition of similar homes.</li>
        <li>Features that distinguish the property.</li>
        <li>Potential improvements that may strengthen its presentation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This evaluation can help determine whether a project is likely to be worthwhile or whether the money would be better retained for the seller’s next purchase or move.</p>
      <p>Remember that no improvement guarantees a particular selling price or return on investment. Real estate values depend on many factors, including market conditions and buyer demand.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">15. Develop a Preparation Plan Before Hiring Contractors</h2>
      <p>Once you have identified the improvements worth considering, establish a realistic timeline. Allow enough time for estimates, scheduling, materials, repairs, cleaning, photography, and the final preparation of the property.</p>
      <p>For larger projects, obtain written estimates and verify that contractors have the appropriate licensing and insurance where required.</p>
      <p>If work requires permits, confirm the applicable requirements before beginning.</p>
      <p>Avoid starting multiple projects simultaneously without considering how they may affect one another.</p>
      <p>For example, painting before flooring installation may be preferable in some situations, while landscaping and exterior cleaning may be scheduled closer to the photography date.</p>
      <p>A coordinated plan can help prevent duplicated work, unnecessary expenses, and delays.</p>
      <p>It is also important to leave room in the budget for unexpected repairs.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-[var(--sage-ink)] pt-6">The Final Word: Spend Strategically, Not Emotionally</h2>
      <p>Preparing a home for sale is not about creating a perfect property or making every room look brand new.</p>
      <p>It is about identifying the improvements that will help the home present itself well to prospective buyers while protecting the seller from unnecessary expenses.</p>
      <p>A clean interior, attractive curb appeal, functioning systems, well-maintained landscaping, and thoughtful cosmetic updates can all contribute to a positive impression.</p>
      <p>Major renovations, on the other hand, should be approached with careful consideration of their cost, timing, and relevance to the local market.</p>
      <p>Every property has different strengths and weaknesses. The best preparation plan recognizes those differences and focuses on the improvements that make the most sense for that particular home.</p>
      <p>Thinking about selling your home? Before investing in renovations or hiring contractors, consider having a local real estate professional evaluate your property and discuss a preparation strategy. Understanding what buyers are looking for in your neighborhood can help you prioritize improvements, manage your budget, and approach the selling process with greater confidence.</p>
      <p>Sometimes the smartest investment isn’t the biggest renovation. It’s knowing which improvements are worth making—and which ones aren’t.</p>
